network-manager-gpclient (2.0.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * RPM packages for Fedora 43, 44 and 45, RHEL/AlmaLinux/Rocky 10 and
    openSUSE Leap 16.0 and Tumbleweed, for x86_64 and aarch64, in a signed
    RPM repository next to the apt repository (dnf and zypper). The SELinux
    module for Fedora and EL is included
  * Support for Ubuntu 24.10 was dropped (it is end of life): no new
    packages are built for it. Supported Ubuntu releases: 22.04, 24.04 and
    26.04, on amd64 and arm64. The packages already published for 24.10 stay
    in the apt repository
  * New setting nm-routing (experimental): NetworkManager sets the tunnel's
    routes, DNS and default route from what the gateway sends, instead of
    vpnc-script. Set it in the GNOME and Plasma editors
  * Translations of the GNOME editors, the authentication dialog and the
    Plasma editor into 36 languages. The translations are machine-made and
    no human translator has reviewed them. Corrections are welcome: see
    docs/TRANSLATIONS.md
  * SAML login in the authentication dialog (vpn.data saml-auth=dialog,
    closes #125): the dialog starts gpauth in your desktop session and hands
    the result to gpclient. New settings for a client certificate (usercert,
    userkey, cert-pass; closes #134) and a custom HIP script (hip-script;
    closes #65), all in the editors with a file chooser. The Plasma
    authentication dialog supports interactive logins (thanks to PR #33)
  * Password and passphrase fields in the editors have a storage menu (this
    user only, all users, ask every time), and the Plasma editor uses
    plasma-nm's own password field. The authentication dialog also works in
    GNOME Shell and with nmcli --ask. The service no longer changes the
    profile while NetworkManager can still ask for secrets (closes #120)
  * New tunnel MTU setting (vpn.data mtu) and a connect timeout field
    (vpn.timeout) in the editors
  * Import and export of connections as NetworkManager keyfiles in the GNOME
    and Plasma editors, and with nmcli connection import/export
  * The GNOME and Plasma editors check the fields with one shared rule file
    (a DNS entry that is not an IP address is refused); the GTK4 editor
    requires the gateway (closes #61)
  * Fix routing and DNS: split-DNS domains are sent to NetworkManager as
    routing-only domains; the routes that NetworkManager applies
    are not sent back to it; never-default also drops the IPv6 default
    route (closes #109); ignore-auto-routes also empties the IPv6 split
    lists (closes #152); the routing hook also runs on disconnect (closes
    #155); and vpnc-script's disconnect runs after gpclient had to be killed
    (closes #162). The IPv4 address, gateway and routes are sent to
    NetworkManager in the right byte order, and the gateway is the VPN
    server's address
  * IPv6: a tunnel with an IPv6 address gets an Ip6Config, an IPv6-only
    tunnel is supported with ipv6.method auto, and an IPv6 VPN server
    address is accepted. An IPv4 tunnel fails when ipv4.method is not auto
    (closes #132)
  * The service reports the tunnel when the vpnc hook says it is up, not by
    polling, and names the tunnel gp-<uuid8>. gpclient output is read from
    its JSON log records, so other text cannot be taken for a prompt. The
    service tracks the VPN state, reports a silent gpclient exit,
    stops only its own gpclient and gpauth, and no longer fails when a
    Disconnect arrives during the OpenSSL retry
  * Gateway handling: gpclient gets --gateway for a gateway picked in the
    editor, and a new SAML login runs before the retry without --gateway
    (closes #138). When gpclient prints DNS settings only in its log, the
    service reads them as a fallback (closes #37)
  * The service writes the connection profile over NetworkManager's D-Bus
    API. A one-time code stored alone is dropped, and a blanked one is not
    sent again at every connection
  * Security: only root may call the service; profile values stay out of
    gpclient options; the desktop user is taken from logind, not from the
    first logged-in user (closes #51); passwords and one-time codes stay out
    of the log; the desktop session environment is not copied into the root
    gpclient; the browser profile of the wrapper is in a private directory;
    a custom HIP script must be root-owned in safe directories; the unit
    runs with systemd sandboxing and starts on demand; the D-Bus policy is
    in /usr/share
  * Fix the SAML browser: a snap Chromium profile works, a failed start is
    reported, and on KDE the default browser starts
  * The packages carry gpclient and gpauth 3.2.0 (upstream was 3.1.0 for
    part of this cycle); gpservice is no longer built or installed (closes
    #39). The menu entry of gpgui is hidden. The unit is installed in
    /usr/lib/systemd/system. Ubuntu 22.04: install python3-sdbus with sudo
    pip3 (closes #30). The postinst and postrm scripts match the running
    service by its command line (closes #43)
  * Releases: the version is now native (2.0.0, with no Debian revision).
    The release build fails when the tag differs from debian/changelog,
    verifies the checksums and provenance of every release before signing,
    attests the packages, and adds an SBOM of the Rust dependencies; the apt
    repository publishes only the newest version of each package (closes
    #44), has by-hash files and no MD5 or SHA1. The release notes keep the
    table of downloads. Pull requests get installable test packages that
    scripts/install-pr-build.sh checks before it installs them
  * Build and test changes: cargo build --locked everywhere, actions pinned
    to commit SHAs, CodeQL, zizmor and Scorecard workflows, ruff and mypy,
    shellcheck of every script, lintian errors fixed, coverage of the
    service gated in CI, upgrade and end-to-end tests, and a README demo as
    animated WebP. One Dockerfile builds every Ubuntu release (closes
    #158). gpclient and gpauth are built with cargo auditable, so the
    binaries carry their dependency list (closes #159). The package builds
    use vendored crates and need no network; a workflow checks that two
    builds give the same packages (closes #160). The openSUSE builds retry
    a zypper refresh that a stale mirror breaks

 -- WMP <example@example.org>  Thu, 29 Oct 2026 00:09:04 +0000

network-manager-gpclient (1.5.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fix the wrong gateway being selected from a paged gateway list (closes
    #25): gpclient's list redraws only the rows that changed, which the
    service did not follow, so it pressed Enter on whatever was highlighted
    (Ireland preferred, France North connected). The service now reads
    gpclient's output through a model of the terminal screen
  * Cache the portal's whole gateway list, not only the first page: a list
    longer than one page is read page by page (PageDown, then Home) before
    the gateway is selected. New vpn.data gateway-list-count marks the cached
    list as complete; the list is read again when the portal reports another
    number of gateways
  * Fix the interactive login: a username prompt under an RSA banner was
    taken for a one-time code, a password label mentioning "user" for a
    username, ordinary passwords ("instance", "encoded") were never answered
    from the profile because one-time keywords matched as substrings, a
    failed nmcli call left the one-time code in the profile, and the walk
    through a paged gateway list stopped at a substring match (gw-1 selected
    gw-10)
  * Fix the SAML browser for usernames with a dot, EntraID usernames
    (user@domain) and upper case ones; on KDE the default browser is started
    through xdg-open; the wrapper logs more
  * browser-wrapper identifies the desktop user by the UID passed by the
    service, never by a user name, and never starts the browser as root
  * Report the DNS servers and search domains the gateway pushes to
    NetworkManager in Ip4Config (closes #15). They used to be applied by
    vpnc-script alone, straight to systemd-resolved, so NetworkManager knew
    the tunnel only as an assumed device without DNS and wiped the VPN
    resolver on its next DNS recalculation (an IPv6 router advertisement on
    the physical interface was enough) while the tunnel stayed up. The
    connect.d hook now records INTERNAL_IP4_DNS / CISCO_DEF_DOMAIN /
    CISCO_SPLIT_DNS in /run/nm-gpclient/dns-state for the service to read
  * vpn.data dns still overrides the pushed servers; dns-domains are added to
    the pushed search domains
  * Packages for arm64 (closes #21) next to amd64, for every Ubuntu release
  * Packages for Ubuntu 24.10
  * The Plasma editor stays one package, network-manager-gpclient-plasma,
    built for the Plasma of each release: Qt5 on Ubuntu 22.04 and 24.04, Qt6
    on Ubuntu 24.10 and 26.04. The former test packages of pull requests,
    network-manager-gpclient-plasma-5 and -plasma-6, were never released: the
    new package Breaks them, so apt full-upgrade removes them and
    apt install network-manager-gpclient-plasma installs the editor
  * New network-manager-gpclient-plasma-6 for KDE neon (Ubuntu 24.04 with
    Plasma 6 from archive.neon.kde.org), built against the neon repository for
    amd64 only; it conflicts with the Qt5 network-manager-gpclient-plasma of
    Ubuntu 24.04 (only one Plasma generation can use the plugin), which needs
    plasma-nm (<< 4:6) and is refused by apt on Plasma 6. Thanks to the idea of PR #16
  * Dependencies list the t64 library names next to the older ones, so the
    packages install on releases with and without the 64-bit time_t rename
  * The release notes start with a table of downloads by Ubuntu release and
    architecture; pull requests get installable test packages

 -- WMP <example@example.org>  Fri, 02 Oct 2026 12:00:00 +0200

network-manager-gpclient (1.4.1-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fix the connection never leaving "activating" when two other tun-based
    VPNs are already up (closes #13): tunnel detection no longer polls a
    fixed gpd0/tun0/tun1 list, which could not see our own tunnel once it
    landed on tun2 or higher, so NetworkManager's vpn.timeout tore the
    connection down. The candidates are read from /sys/class/net instead,
    and any number of foreign tunnels may be active
  * Attribute the tunnel to gpclient by the file descriptors it holds
    (iff: in /proc/PID/fdinfo), so another VPN activated during
    authentication is not adopted as ours

 -- WMP <example@example.org>  Wed, 26 Aug 2026 12:00:00 +0200

network-manager-gpclient (1.4.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fix portal addresses: --gateway is no longer forced to the server address,
    which made gpclient abort with "Cannot find gateway specified" for every
    real portal (closes #7). The gateway prompt is answered by the service:
    vpn.data preferred-gateway if it matches, otherwise the portal's first
    proposal, with a fallback when a configured gateway is gone
  * Cache the portal's gateway list in the profile (vpn.data gateway-list)
    after a successful connection and offer it in the connection editors
  * New connection options: vpn.data as-gateway, vpn.data preferred-gateway
  * Fix the authentication browser closing after ~8 s: the new
    /usr/libexec/gpclient/browser-wrapper waits for gpauth to finish instead
    of guessing from tunnel interfaces (GP_AUTH_TIMEOUT overrides the 300 s
    safety timeout); edge-wrapper stays as a compatibility shim
  * Support any browser (edge, firefox, chrome, chromium, default) and import
    the user's session environment, so browsers other than Edge actually open
    a window on Wayland (closes #7)
  * Auth dialog: answer SAML connections with an empty secret set instead of
    an error, so opening the connection editor no longer waits out
    NetworkManager's 25 s secrets timeout (closes #8)
  * Expose Preferred gateway, Address is a gateway, Username and
    Authentication in the GNOME and Plasma connection editors
  * Work around portals that need legacy TLS renegotiation (closes #2): retry
    once with gpclient's --fix-openssl when it reports
    "unsafe legacy renegotiation disabled", then store fix-openssl=true in the
    profile so the failed first attempt does not repeat. New connection option
    vpn.data fix-openssl=auto|true|false, exposed in the editors
  * Emit the STOPPED state after a failure, so NetworkManager reports the error
    immediately instead of waiting out its connect timeout

 -- WMP <example@example.org>  Sun, 26 Jul 2026 20:00:00 +0200

network-manager-gpclient (1.3.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Support portals with standard login / RSA token challenges (closes #6):
    run gpclient under a PTY, detect its interactive prompts and answer
    them from stored connection secrets or interactively via the
    NetworkManager SecretsRequired/NewSecrets flow
  * Add GNOME/nm-applet auth dialog (/usr/libexec/nm-gpclient-auth-dialog)
  * New connection options: vpn.data username, vpn.data auth-mode=credentials,
    vpn.secrets password
  * Add unit tests for prompt detection (make test-unit)

